From 01ff2709fdf92702663a479bcd1c6036515157cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yibo Wang <2024312277@stu.hit.edu.cn>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 11:11:58 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5: quiet repeated handover interrupts
X-Original-Commit: 4f92b7d7df625c2800c042e58330d4eb8dc33cbe
X-Original-Committer: Yibo Wang <2024312277@stu.hit.edu.cn>
X-Original-Committer-Date: 2026-10-08T11:11:58-05:00

The SM8850 ADSP firmware re-asserts the handover SMP2P bit
periodically, roughly one interrupt every few seconds, and the
driver logs an error for every repeat after the first. On this
device that floods dmesg with thousands of lines and drowns
everything else, while the reference (vendor) driver handles
repeats without complaint.

Demote the repeat notice to dev_dbg. Rate limiting would not
help here - the natural rate sits below the ratelimit threshold.

Verified on the device: zero occurrences after an hour where the
previous kernel logged thousands; adsp/cdsp/soccp all healthy.
---
 drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
index 241478ea29782a1cb0ad48a165798f82fccb7953..49c13c8596cafe575f3bea0dc6477917949a406d 100644
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -165,7 +165,13 @@ static irqreturn_t q6v5_handover_interrupt(int irq, void *data)
 	struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5 = data;
 
 	if (q6v5->handover_issued) {
-		dev_err(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
+		/*
+		 * Some firmware (e.g. the SM8850 ADSP) re-asserts the handover
+		 * SMP2P bit periodically; the reference driver handles repeats
+		 * without complaint. Keep the trace but out of the error log,
+		 * where it drowns everything else.
+		 */
+		dev_dbg(q6v5->dev, "handover re-signaled\n");
 		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	}
 
